        "problem": "Express $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061u\\operatorname{cos}\u2061v-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061u\\operatorname{sin}\u2061v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x+\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
